RECURSIVE FUNCTION:
- Function calls itself.
eg:
int fun()
{
printf("hello");
fun();
}
output: Infinite times hello will be printed.
- which causes a segmentation fault.
- Because, when the allocated memory for stackframes gets full, it leads to stack overflow.
- We can stop this by using a base condition, it will only run until the condition is false.
eg:
Q) WAP to print 5 to 1 numbers
int main() {
static int count = 5;
printf("%d", count);
count--;
if(count != 0)
main();
}

output: 5 4 3 2 1
- by using static keyword the value is stored in data segment.
- And updates everytime the new stackframe is created.
- once the condition is false, it goes to previous stackframe, where the function is called.
- check whether any statements present below the function call, performs them and goes to previous stackframe.
- if not also, goes to previous stack frames.
- continues the process till the first stackframe and returns output.
- all the stackframes are automatically deleted.
- loops are faster than recursive function.
- Recursive function is used to store previous stackframe data address.
